00:10:18.733 --> 00:10:23.033
With all that being said, I'm
going to dive into a case study

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:26.600
of automating biomarker
discovery from FFPE samples

00:10:26.667 --> 00:10:28.933
using the i-Series
Automated Workstation

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:34.333
and our FormaPure DNA kits as well
as the Illumina TruSeq Exome kit.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:38.067
The National Cancer Institute
defines a biomarker

00:10:38.133 --> 00:10:41.167
as a biological molecule found
in blood or other fluids

00:10:41.233 --> 00:10:44.967
that is indicative of a treatment
or disease conditions.

00:10:45.033 --> 00:10:50.600
Biomarkers can take the form of DNA
variants, RNA variants, or proteins.

00:10:50.667 --> 00:10:53.300
For DNA variants, they come
in a wide variety of flavors.

00:10:53.367 --> 00:10:55.533
You can have single nucleotide
polymorphisms, insertions,

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:58.967
deletions, copy number variants.

00:10:59.033 --> 00:11:01.367
So one of the things that researchers
really want to understand

00:11:01.433 --> 00:11:07.200
is what is the molecular drivers of
cancer in this particular research area?

00:11:07.267 --> 00:11:10.967
Another thing you'll find very
commonly in oncology is FFPE DNA.

00:11:11.033 --> 00:11:16.467
FFPE stands for fixed formalin
paraffin embedded tissue.

00:11:16.533 --> 00:11:20.233
Effectively what happens is when
you go in and have a biopsy done,

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:27.733
the tissue is fixed in formalin
and then embedded in paraffin.

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:31.067
And this allows the tissue sample

00:11:31.133 --> 00:11:35.500
to be stored at room temperature
for decades fairly cheaply.

00:11:35.567 --> 00:11:39.167
So it's incredibly easy to perform,
and it's quite economical as well.

00:11:39.233 --> 00:11:41.567
It's just a few dollars per block.

00:11:41.633 --> 00:11:45.433
Part of the difficulties of working with
it include difficult extractions of DNA.

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:48.933
The DNA is frequently damaged
due to a variety of things

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:52.967
including double stranded breaks,
deamination, and pyrimidine dimers.

00:11:53.033 --> 00:11:55.000
And then you also get variable yield

00:11:55.067 --> 00:11:57.433
due to an uneven distribution
of sample in the block.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:11:59.933
So you don't necessarily know
when you're going through

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.200
and slicing the block into
your ten micron slices

00:12:02.267 --> 00:12:06.533
where exactly that tissue
is going to be located.

00:12:07.067 --> 00:12:10.200
Needless to say it's not everyones
favorite material for working with,

00:12:10.267 --> 00:12:13.100
but it's what we have.

00:12:13.167 --> 00:12:17.067
With that, Beckman Coulter just
recently released a new kit

00:12:17.133 --> 00:12:22.567
called FormaPure DNA for
extracting DNA from FFPE material.

00:12:22.633 --> 00:12:25.100
This is the entirety of the workflow.

00:12:25.167 --> 00:12:28.567
So we take our slice and
we deparaffinize it

00:12:28.633 --> 00:12:31.433
by heating it up in mineral oil.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:33.733
We then add our lysis buffer
and Proteinase K

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:35.967
and digest the tissue away,

00:12:36.033 --> 00:12:39.667
perform our decrosslinking by
heating the sample up some more.

00:12:39.733 --> 00:12:43.500
And then we add our RNase A
to digest away all the RNA,

00:12:43.567 --> 00:12:47.500
and then we use our solid phase
reversible immobilization technology

00:12:47.567 --> 00:12:51.700
or SPRI beads to bind the
DNA, remove it from solution,

00:12:51.767 --> 00:12:54.233
wash it, and then elute.

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:56.700
So as you can see, it's an
extremely compact workflow

00:12:56.767 --> 00:12:59.200
and does not require any spin columns.

00:12:59.267 --> 00:13:02.300
It generally works quite nicely.

00:13:02.367 --> 00:13:04.133
So nine steps.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:07.433
There's one optional centrifuge
step, no Xylene required,

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:09.400
and just three temperatures.

00:13:09.467 --> 00:13:11.633
So it's not a terribly
complex workflow.

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:16.367
It makes it quite easy on automation
specialists such as myself.

00:13:16.433 --> 00:13:20.500
So this is just some manual extraction
data that colleague, Jung Do,

00:13:20.567 --> 00:13:24.367
who's the internal application
scientist for reagents generated.

00:13:24.433 --> 00:13:26.367
So as you can see here,

00:13:26.433 --> 00:13:33.633
compairing FP DNA to Supplier Q
yield with Horizon breast, lung,

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:36.600
large intestine, liver, and lung,

00:13:36.667 --> 00:13:40.133
we were able to get
comparable or better yields,

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:43.733
both in terms of the quantity
of DNA that was isolated

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:46.367
as well as the quality of the DNA,

00:13:46.433 --> 00:13:52.000
per nanodrop on the
chart on the bottom right.

00:13:52.067 --> 00:13:54.633
The next question we
wanted to address

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:57.633
is because we had heard from a
variety of customers is well,

00:13:57.700 --> 00:13:59.333
that's all well and good,

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:01.400
but what if I wanted to
do a split extraction?

00:14:01.467 --> 00:14:06.333
What if I wanted to get DNA and
RNA out of the same sample?

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:11.167
So Jung went back and began
working on an expanded workflow.

00:14:11.233 --> 00:14:15.967
So the split workflow is
shown here graphically,

00:14:16.033 --> 00:14:22.167
but it's very similar to
the DNA only workflow.

00:14:22.233 --> 00:14:27.400
We form the deparaffinization and
lysis and the protein SK treatment,

00:14:27.467 --> 00:14:32.533
and then the lysate itself is
split into two different plates.

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:36.733
And then we proceed to go through
and do a DNA workflow on top,

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:39.600
and an RNA workflow on the bottom.

00:14:39.667 --> 00:14:42.133
So we allow this to actually isolate

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:45.433
maybe total nucleic
acid from one sample.

00:14:46.167 --> 00:14:49.600
This is some comparative RNA
yields comparing Supplier Q,

00:14:49.667 --> 00:14:51.933
Supplier O, and Supplier A.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:55.433
We also looked at the Peltier
orbital shaker on these

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:58.267
so you can see our RNA
yields are quite good

00:14:58.333 --> 00:15:02.233
that we're getting from
stomach and from -

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:04.567
obviously, not as good from
breast or lung or liver,

00:15:04.633 --> 00:15:08.033
but a lot of this is block dependent.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:10.500
So I think the yields here

00:15:10.567 --> 00:15:14.167
are certainly more than sufficient
for most NGS applications.

00:15:14.233 --> 00:15:16.200
The other thing we've noticed,

00:15:16.267 --> 00:15:19.233
or we've shown at a variety
of different locations,

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:21.033
not just our internal lab,

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:24.333
is that the RNA we're getting
is actually more intact

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:27.500
than RNA that's derived
from comparable kits.

00:15:27.567 --> 00:15:29.667
So you can see here

00:15:29.733 --> 00:15:36.133
these are bioanalyzer RNA
nanotraces of the RNA

00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:39.100
that was derived from using
a FormaPure DNA kit.

00:15:39.167 --> 00:15:41.200
The blue traces you're seeing

00:15:41.267 --> 00:15:45.467
in each of the four images that
you're seeing here are from our kit,

00:15:45.533 --> 00:15:50.300
and the red traces you're seeing
here are from Supplier Q's kit.

00:15:50.367 --> 00:15:52.500
So as you can see,
we're actually getting

00:15:52.567 --> 00:15:57.700
more higher molecular weight RNA
species with our chemistry,

00:15:57.767 --> 00:16:00.633
which this is pretty exciting stuff.

00:16:01.267 --> 00:16:04.300
The other thing we did is we
went through and verified

00:16:04.367 --> 00:16:07.233
that the RNA we're using is
actually NGS compatible.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:10.100
We actually ran through the manual -

00:16:10.167 --> 00:16:13.567
we ran through, manually, the
Illumina TruSeq RNA Access kit.

00:16:13.633 --> 00:16:16.667
So this is some more work that
was done by a colleague, Jung.

00:16:16.733 --> 00:16:22.100
On my left, your right,
probably, side of the screen,

00:16:22.167 --> 00:16:26.333
you'll see bioanalyzer traces of
various points of library prep

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:28.467
and then some sequencing metrics

00:16:28.533 --> 00:16:32.300
that Jung generated in house
on our NextSeq platform.

00:16:32.367 --> 00:16:35.933
As you can see, the insert link
distribution was pretty tight.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.600
The majority of the sample was
coming to the coding region,

00:16:38.667 --> 00:16:41.033
and our transcript coverage
was fairly uniform,

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:43.567
meaning that we're not biasing -

00:16:43.633 --> 00:16:46.000
the library preparation isn't biased

00:16:46.067 --> 00:16:48.167
towards one end of the
RNA or the other.

00:16:48.233 --> 00:16:50.367
So that was some pretty solid data.

00:16:52.333 --> 00:16:57.333
For my part, what I did was we
put together an entire workflow

00:16:57.400 --> 00:17:03.967
utilizing our chemistry and our
liquid handler for the DNA extraction

00:17:04.033 --> 00:17:06.500
from the FFPE blocks,

00:17:06.567 --> 00:17:10.033
and utilizing the
Illumina TruSeq Exome kit

00:17:10.100 --> 00:17:13.300
on our liquid handler for the
actual ingest library prep.

00:17:13.367 --> 00:17:17.067
As you can see here, we can certainly
provide you with a section

00:17:17.133 --> 00:17:22.400
and options through our fellow
op co at Leica with Microtome,

00:17:22.467 --> 00:17:26.333
and then we also utilize the
molecular devices SpectraMax i3

00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:28.567
for our four metric
assays for concentration,

00:17:28.633 --> 00:17:35.133
we determined after
nucleic acid extractions.

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:38.300
So we did the block sectioning
on the Leica Mictrotome,

00:17:38.367 --> 00:17:43.167
we then extracted the DNA using
our FormaPure DNA kit,

00:17:43.233 --> 00:17:45.933
performed a QC of the DNA on
the liquid handler,

00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:48.333
then did our library prep on
the same platform,

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:53.067
and then finally did NGS QC on
the platform as well.

00:17:53.133 --> 00:17:55.567
So this is the deck configuration

00:17:55.633 --> 00:17:57.733
of the instrument that I used
for this work.

00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:02.267
This is a Biomek i-Series i7, dual
hybrid with a multichannel pod

00:18:02.333 --> 00:18:04.333
and a stamp pod.

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:06.600
So the multichannel pod
in here, in this case,

00:18:06.667 --> 00:18:10.967
has our new 1200 microliter
pipetting head on it.

00:18:11.033 --> 00:18:15.933
It is also equipped with an
automated thermocycler, the ATC.

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:21.100
We also have the ability, incidentally,
to do a Biometra TRobot or Inheco ODTC

00:18:21.167 --> 00:18:23.533
if you want additional walk away time

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:26.000
than putting your thermocycler
right on the deck.

00:18:26.067 --> 00:18:29.533
And we also used a Beckman orbital
shaker and one Inheco static Peltier

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:32.133
for keeping our enzyme
master mixes cool.

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:37.000
So as you can see, the extra deck
capacity really does help a lot.

00:18:37.367 --> 00:18:40.333
The other thing is these methods
that we've developed in house

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:43.200
is our Demonstrated Method Interface,

00:18:43.267 --> 00:18:47.167
which is a three-part
software interface.

00:18:47.233 --> 00:18:49.100
The first part of the software

00:18:49.167 --> 00:18:52.333
would be our Biomek
Method Launcher offering.

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:57.533
What we've come to the realization
over the course of our last few years

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:00.300
is that the majority of our
users are operators,

00:19:00.367 --> 00:19:02.333
they're not necessarily programmers.

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:05.500
And so Biomek Method Launcher was
designed to give our operators

00:19:05.567 --> 00:19:07.933
the ability to just
go up to an interface

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:10.267
and select which method
they want to run

00:19:10.333 --> 00:19:13.100
and not have to dive into the
complexities of the Biomek software

00:19:13.167 --> 00:19:15.467
if they didn't wish to.

00:19:15.533 --> 00:19:18.967
So they would go to the Launcher,
they would select from FormaPure DNA,

00:19:19.033 --> 00:19:22.733
or they would select the NGS
application of interest,

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:27.533
and then number two, the Method
Option Selecter, would then pop up.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:30.533
So this is where they're able to
go and configure the workflow

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:34.000
to match the number of
samples they're processing.

00:19:34.067 --> 00:19:37.233
If they want to stop and
approve stop points,

00:19:37.300 --> 00:19:40.667
this is where they would set that up.

00:19:40.733 --> 00:19:42.933
And then once they've started the run,

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:45.267
then our Guided Labware
Setup will go through

00:19:45.333 --> 00:19:49.633
and step them through where on the
deck to put all the tip boxes, plates,

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:53.100
and where to fill in
arrays and troughs

00:19:53.167 --> 00:19:56.000
with the various
components that they need.

00:19:56.067 --> 00:19:59.033
So this is really trying
to help the operators

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:01.367
come to the point where
they can go through

00:20:01.433 --> 00:20:05.733
and set the instrument up with
confidence and reduced errors.

00:20:12.633 --> 00:20:16.267
For our FormaPure DNA method,

00:20:16.333 --> 00:20:19.600
I've got sort of a workflow
diagram up top.

00:20:19.667 --> 00:20:22.967
For a manual protocol, we have
the dewax, lysis, decross,

00:20:23.033 --> 00:20:26.167
nuclease treatment, bind,
two washes, and elution.

00:20:26.233 --> 00:20:28.167
For the automation protocol,

00:20:28.233 --> 00:20:31.367
we basically do all that as
one general workflow.

00:20:31.433 --> 00:20:34.700
There is approximately 15 to
20 minutes of hands-on time

00:20:34.767 --> 00:20:36.733
for setting the method up,

00:20:36.800 --> 00:20:41.600
and then there are a variety of times
that you're seeing listed here.

00:20:41.667 --> 00:20:46.100
So if you're running through
and doing the dewax, lysis,

00:20:46.167 --> 00:20:48.200
and decross on deck,

00:20:48.267 --> 00:20:50.500
that's approximately four
hours and forty-five minutes

00:20:50.567 --> 00:20:55.833
for up to 96 DNA samples,

00:20:56.033 --> 00:21:00.567
and one hour and thirty-three minutes
of the actual nucleic acid, bind,

00:21:00.700 --> 00:21:03.067
wash, elute part of the method.

00:21:03.133 --> 00:21:07.633
So effectively, you're looking at
about six hours and fifteen minutes,

00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:09.967
approximately, for 96 DNA samples,

00:21:10.033 --> 00:21:13.133
or six hours and thirty minutes
for up to 96 RNA only,

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:15.733
or if we were performing
a split workflow,

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:21.067
96 DNA and 96 RNA simultaneously
from the same sections.

00:21:21.133 --> 00:21:23.700
So one technician with one Biomek

00:21:23.767 --> 00:21:26.133
in approximately an hour and
a half of hands-on time

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:28.133
could do up to 500 samples a week

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:30.300
with the automation method
that we've developed

00:21:30.367 --> 00:21:32.433
as it currently stands.

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:34.700
So, not too shabby.

00:21:36.567 --> 00:21:39.667
For the biomarker experiment
that we performed here,

00:21:39.733 --> 00:21:45.033
we had eight technical replicates of
Horizon HD200 reference materials.

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:47.600
This is the multiplex FFPE
reference material

00:21:47.667 --> 00:21:50.467
that Horizon Diagnostics sells.

00:21:50.533 --> 00:21:54.700
As well as five different FFPE
block samples from Asterand -

00:21:54.767 --> 00:21:58.967
two breast, two lung, and a liver
at ages three to five years.

00:21:59.033 --> 00:22:01.433
And we had four technical
replicates for each of those.

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:07.033
We had one ten micron curl for each
of those technical replicates.

00:22:09.233 --> 00:22:11.500
So here's our FFPE DNA yields

00:22:11.567 --> 00:22:17.633
as calculated from a PicoGreen
setup on the Biomek i-Series.

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:21.333
So Horizon samples, we had one
sample, Horizon number four,

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:25.000
where there was not as
much material in the tube

00:22:25.067 --> 00:22:27.600
as there was the other samples -
but I went ahead and ran it anyway.

00:22:27.667 --> 00:22:29.633
I don't know if they were
running to the end of the block

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:31.600
when they were sectioning that one.

00:22:31.667 --> 00:22:33.600
As you can see, for all
the other samples,

00:22:33.667 --> 00:22:38.033
we're getting well in excess of four
nanograms of double stranded DNA

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:41.667
out of each of our Horizon samples,
which was quite heartening.

00:22:41.733 --> 00:22:45.333
Some of which were quite a bit
higher than 400 nanograms.

00:22:45.400 --> 00:22:47.600
Then as you can see from
the rest of the samples -

00:22:47.667 --> 00:22:52.233
76 of 96 were our lung samples.

00:22:53.533 --> 00:22:55.600
Sorry, breast.

00:22:55.667 --> 00:22:57.600
As you can see here,

00:22:57.667 --> 00:23:00.367
theres quite a bit of difference
in the yields we're getting.

00:23:00.433 --> 00:23:03.100
We see this all the time with
FFPE material, that there is -

00:23:03.167 --> 00:23:05.233
going from section to section
and from block to block,

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:07.933
we can see quite a bit of
difference in yield.

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:10.133
For our breast and liver,

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:13.700
so breast was 110 and 121
and our liver was 107.

00:23:13.767 --> 00:23:16.533
Again, we are seeing some
variability in the yield,

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:18.500
but certainly more than enough

00:23:18.567 --> 00:23:22.667
for what we need to do for
our NGS library prep.

00:23:24.267 --> 00:23:28.333
We also looked at a few
of the technical replicates

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:30.500
on the Agilent tape stations.

00:23:30.567 --> 00:23:33.100
This is a genomic tape -
genomic screen tape

00:23:33.167 --> 00:23:35.600
that was run on our Agilent 2200.

00:23:35.667 --> 00:23:38.567
As you can see here, our
Horizon sample obviously had

00:23:38.633 --> 00:23:41.367
the highest DNA integrity
number at 9.6,

00:23:41.433 --> 00:23:43.400
but that's not terribly surprising

00:23:43.467 --> 00:23:46.733
given that Horizon material
is not terribly old

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:49.733
by comparison to the
rest of the samples,

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:51.533
and it's probably been fixed

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:54.367
in a more gentle fashion
than the Asterand blocks.

00:23:54.433 --> 00:23:57.100
So our DNA integrity numbers
for the remaining samples

00:23:57.167 --> 00:24:00.033
were running from 2.2 to 3.6,

00:24:00.100 --> 00:24:03.100
but obviously, more than
enough material to go through

00:24:03.167 --> 00:24:07.133
and do our library prep on.

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:11.267
In a separate experiment, we're
looking at the split protocol.

00:24:11.333 --> 00:24:17.300
So this was doing the FFPE DNA and
RNA on the Biomek i-Series system.

00:24:17.367 --> 00:24:19.100
So we have the DNA yields,

00:24:19.167 --> 00:24:22.667
and a size distribution
of the DNA on top,

00:24:22.733 --> 00:24:25.967
and then for the RNA, we have
the yields on the bottom

00:24:26.033 --> 00:24:28.300
as well as the sides distribution.

00:24:28.367 --> 00:24:30.467
So this is some very early access data

00:24:30.533 --> 00:24:32.567
we just developed
this a few weeks ago.

00:24:32.633 --> 00:24:35.933
More work to come on this
particular application,

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:38.633
but just to show that we can do it.

00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:44.267
TruSeq Exome, for anyone who is
familiar with the workflow,

00:24:44.333 --> 00:24:49.033
it's a much more complicated workflow
than our extraction chemistry.

00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:51.667
You start out with a DNA
fragmentation at cleanup

00:24:51.733 --> 00:24:53.967
followed by end repair and
size selection,

00:24:54.033 --> 00:24:56.733
atailing adapter ligation,
PCR and post-PCR cleanup,

00:24:56.800 --> 00:25:00.267
and that sort of section one up
to the end of library construction.

00:25:00.333 --> 00:25:03.033
Then you quantify the
individual libraries

00:25:03.100 --> 00:25:05.600
and you pull them for your
hybridization capture,

00:25:05.667 --> 00:25:08.467
and then theres two rounds
of hybridization capture,

00:25:08.533 --> 00:25:11.933
followed by enrichment PCR
and enrichment PCR cleanup.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:13.567
Our automation method

00:25:13.633 --> 00:25:16.500
basically divides those
into three major sections.

00:25:16.567 --> 00:25:20.067
On the first day,
you would run a section one

00:25:20.133 --> 00:25:24.133
and get that up through your end
of library construction.

00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:28.600
And then section two you
would run on day two,

00:25:28.667 --> 00:25:30.700
going into the second hybridization,

00:25:30.767 --> 00:25:33.733
which requires a minimum of 14 and
a half hours of hybridization.

00:25:33.800 --> 00:25:38.233
And then section three is what you
would finish up on the third day,

00:25:38.300 --> 00:25:41.733
and then the times for processing
up to 96 samples or eight pools

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:46.067
is included in the bottom as well
as some setup time estimates.

00:25:46.133 --> 00:25:48.600
Obviously, I didn't include
the hybridization times

00:25:48.667 --> 00:25:50.933
because those were run off-deck.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:54.333
As far as the hybridizations
are concerned,

00:25:54.400 --> 00:25:56.467
that's not really something
we recommend running

00:25:56.533 --> 00:25:58.633
with the on-deck
thermocyclers generally

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:04.033
because the evaporation might
become quite a bit of an issue.

00:26:06.067 --> 00:26:12.200
For our experiment, we had basically
four six plex pools we were running.

00:26:12.267 --> 00:26:17.233
Pool 1 included all of our FFPE
samples as well as one Horizon spike.

00:26:17.300 --> 00:26:19.333
And in the other three pools,

00:26:19.400 --> 00:26:25.367
we replaced the box 76 technical
replicate with Promega genomic DNA

00:26:25.433 --> 00:26:29.067
just to test the workflow,
to give it some -

00:26:29.133 --> 00:26:31.633
you know, this is what pristine
DNA would look like

00:26:31.700 --> 00:26:33.933
when run through the automation.

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:36.000
There were some protocol deviations,

00:26:36.067 --> 00:26:38.100
so Illumina did publish a tech note

00:26:38.167 --> 00:26:40.500
for working with FFPE material
with this kit.

00:26:40.567 --> 00:26:42.633
So we did use 300 nanograms

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:47.200
of FFPE DNA input for the library
construction as opposed to 100.

00:26:47.267 --> 00:26:50.533
Utilized 12 cycles of PCR for
the library amplification

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:55.200
and 500 nanograms of library going
into hybridization capture.

00:26:55.267 --> 00:26:58.700
So those are all per
Illuminas recommendations.

00:26:59.433 --> 00:27:01.700
Here we see some library traces,

00:27:01.767 --> 00:27:04.800
and the final library yields

00:27:04.933 --> 00:27:07.667
that we're looking at for
our individual libraries.

00:27:07.733 --> 00:27:11.333
As you can see on the right, we're
seeing a genomic DNA library,

00:27:11.400 --> 00:27:13.500
one of the Promega
genomic DNA libraries,

00:27:13.567 --> 00:27:16.933
and then on the left, we're
seeing our FFPE libraries.

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:20.133
So we had slightly higher adapter
divers in the FFPE libraries

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:22.667
compared to the genomic library,

00:27:22.733 --> 00:27:26.433
but that is to be expected
given some of the challenges

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:28.933
in working with FFPE material.

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:31.333
It doesn' t necessarily ligate
as well or readily

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:34.333
or amplify as readily
as our genomic DNA

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:38.933
due to the various bits of damage that
occurs during the fixation process.

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:43.300
But our library yields were more
than sufficient for going forward

00:27:43.367 --> 00:27:48.400
into library construction, or
the hybridization capture.

00:27:49.500 --> 00:27:52.700
The traces you're seeing on
this slide are our two pools,

00:27:52.767 --> 00:27:57.733
so we took two of the six plex pools
in through hybridization capture -

00:27:57.800 --> 00:28:00.633
Pool 1 and Pool 2.

00:28:00.700 --> 00:28:02.633
The traces are on the left.

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:07.267
A qPCRs yield calculated on
the right were quite high.

00:28:07.333 --> 00:28:10.733
More than enough material
to go into sequencing.

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:15.000
In our internal applications
lab here in Indianapolis,

00:28:15.067 --> 00:28:18.633
we have both a NextSeq and a
MySeq sequencer from Illumina,

00:28:18.700 --> 00:28:22.200
which is extremely handy for
generating sequencing data.

00:28:22.267 --> 00:28:25.533
We don' t have to rely on a vendor
or an outside source to do so.

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:29.967
So we did a single NextSeq run
with a two by 76 paired-in kit.

00:28:30.033 --> 00:28:33.567
We generated approximately
114 million reads

00:28:33.633 --> 00:28:41.000
with 96 percent of the reads were higher
being path filter and identified.

00:28:41.067 --> 00:28:47.233
We then took the reads and used
the BaseSpace Enrichment 3.0 app,

00:28:47.367 --> 00:28:49.333
which uses the Aligner liner

00:28:49.400 --> 00:28:52.600
and a variety of other
biogrammatics programs

00:28:52.667 --> 00:28:56.200
to map the reads to the reference
genome and to provide

00:28:56.267 --> 00:28:59.033
on target read enrichment numbers.

00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:02.467
So this is the data that
you're seeing here.

00:29:02.533 --> 00:29:06.067
In each of the samples
that constitute Pool 1 -

00:29:06.133 --> 00:29:08.100
so we only sequenced one pool

00:29:08.167 --> 00:29:11.933
in order to get fairly decent depth
of coverage on our one NextSeq run.

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:17.400
Our percent read alignments were
over 90 percent in all cases.

00:29:17.467 --> 00:29:20.000
Our percent padded read enrichment

00:29:20.067 --> 00:29:22.600
was approximately 60 percent
for all samples.

00:29:22.667 --> 00:29:25.933
Padded read enrichment is
the percent of the reads

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:29.167
that are falling within the designated
regions of the TruSeq Exome kit.

00:29:29.233 --> 00:29:32.133
I think there are
approximately 214,000 regions

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:34.400
that are covered in that kit.

00:29:34.467 --> 00:29:36.733
So we would use the depth
file from Illumina

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:42.200
to calculate how many of our map reads
were falling into those regions.

00:29:42.267 --> 00:29:45.533
Of the regions that are in the kit,

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:48.267
approximately 99 percent of
them were covered end-to-end,

00:29:48.333 --> 00:29:52.267
at least to 1x, as you can see
from the graph below.

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:58.333
So that meant that we had fairly
uniform coverage of our regions,

00:29:58.400 --> 00:30:01.367
and that's again
underscored by the graph,

00:30:01.433 --> 00:30:04.367
the uniformity of coverage graph that
you're seeing on the right here.

00:30:04.433 --> 00:30:07.367
This is looking at where the
reads are mapping on average

00:30:07.433 --> 00:30:09.367
for each of the regions,

00:30:09.433 --> 00:30:11.600
and we're seeing
uniformity of coverage

00:30:11.667 --> 00:30:15.633
greater than 90 percent per that data.

00:30:15.700 --> 00:30:18.567
Another thing we looked at was the
mean region depth of coverage.

00:30:18.633 --> 00:30:22.533
So the Horizon sample performed the
best with almost 200x coverage.

00:30:22.600 --> 00:30:26.567
We did see somewhere
around 50x coverage total

00:30:26.633 --> 00:30:32.033
for the breast and the lung,

00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:36.200
and approximately 100x
coverage for the liver sample

00:30:36.267 --> 00:30:38.200
on average across all the regions.

00:30:38.267 --> 00:30:41.300
When we looked at the targeted
region depth of coverage

00:30:41.367 --> 00:30:45.233
and broke it down by 1x,
10x, or 20 to 50x coverage,

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:48.100
the Horizon sample obviously
outperformed all of them.

00:30:48.167 --> 00:30:52.300
So 90 percent of the regions
were covered at 50x or higher.

00:30:52.367 --> 00:30:56.167
The liver sample
performed the next best

00:30:56.233 --> 00:31:00.600
with approximately 70 percent
of the regions covered at 50x or higher.

00:31:00.667 --> 00:31:03.967
The other samples you
can see there as well.

00:31:04.033 --> 00:31:07.100
I believe if we had more sequencing
coverage from MySeq runs

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:08.933
or additional NextSeq runs,

00:31:09.067 --> 00:31:12.067
we could've driven these
numbers up substantially.

00:31:16.033 --> 00:31:18.067
Another thing we wanted to look at

00:31:18.133 --> 00:31:20.233
was what are some of
the SNVs we're seeing

00:31:20.300 --> 00:31:22.100
in each of these samples.

00:31:22.167 --> 00:31:26.367
So we looked at the metrics coming
off of the TruSeq Enrichment app,

00:31:26.433 --> 00:31:29.967
and we generated, compared with
the reference genome,

00:31:30.033 --> 00:31:35.533
anywhere from 28,000 to
65,000 SNVs that we observed.

00:31:35.600 --> 00:31:38.300
The most important thing I
wanted to draw your attention to

00:31:38.367 --> 00:31:41.000
is this SNV transition to
transversion ratio.

00:31:41.067 --> 00:31:43.367
So there's some work that comes
up at the Broad Institute

00:31:43.433 --> 00:31:46.267
that suggests, for a whole
exome experiment,

00:31:46.333 --> 00:31:50.500
part of that ratio should
be approximately 2.8

00:31:50.567 --> 00:31:56.167
as a way of measuring how well
your biogrammatics pipeline was

00:31:56.233 --> 00:31:59.267
mapping the reads to the whole exome.

00:31:59.333 --> 00:32:02.967
And as you can see here, we're
running anywhere from 2.6 to 2.8,

00:32:03.033 --> 00:32:06.300
so I feel like the assembly that
we did of the reads,

00:32:06.367 --> 00:32:11.133
the reference genome, using the
BaseSpace Enrichment 3.0 app,

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:13.333
did a fairly good job of
aligning them properly

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:15.400
and we're not seeing artifacts

00:32:15.467 --> 00:32:18.467
based on the biogrammatics
part of the pipeline.

00:32:20.233 --> 00:32:22.133
Another thing we looked at -

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:24.233
because we had a Horizon
sample on deck -

00:32:24.300 --> 00:32:27.733
was the Horizon sample has
an expected allele frequency

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:29.933
that they publish on their
website for these samples,

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:31.533
which is extremely handy.

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:33.500
It allows you to verify

00:32:33.567 --> 00:32:37.733
that your pipeline for both nucleic
acid extraction and for NGS sample prep

00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:40.533
is actually observing
the allele frequencies

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:43.533
that you should expect to observe
for a variety of different markers.

00:32:43.600 --> 00:32:45.467
As you can see here,

00:32:45.533 --> 00:32:49.700
we did quite well as far as comparing
to our expected allele frequencies,

00:32:49.767 --> 00:32:55.567
compared to what we were able to
identify with the Enrichment v3 app.

00:32:55.633 --> 00:32:58.000
We did have a couple of dropouts.

00:32:58.067 --> 00:33:01.333
Those were in EGFR, but
those were both less than -

00:33:01.400 --> 00:33:05.700
the expected allele frequency
on the Horizon sample

00:33:05.767 --> 00:33:08.433
was less than two percent
for these samples.

00:33:08.500 --> 00:33:11.300
I feel like if we had
additional sequencing coverage,

00:33:11.367 --> 00:33:15.033
that we would be able
to pull those out.

00:33:15.100 --> 00:33:19.967
Additionally, we looked at the
parental line known mutations.

00:33:20.033 --> 00:33:23.300
We were able to identify 17 of
the 19 parental line mutations

00:33:23.367 --> 00:33:25.267
were also detected from our data,

00:33:25.333 --> 00:33:29.000
even though this was a fairly
limited NextSeq sequencing run.

00:33:29.067 --> 00:33:32.500
So I feel like, as far as being
able to find what is there,

00:33:32.567 --> 00:33:35.300
we're on target.

00:33:39.067 --> 00:33:44.233
Finally, we also looked
specifically at the liver sample.

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:47.033
The liver sample was
identified from Asterand

00:33:47.100 --> 00:33:50.100
as being biliary tract carcinoma.

00:33:50.167 --> 00:33:53.467
So biliary tract carcinoma is
a cancer of the bile ducts

00:33:53.533 --> 00:33:57.167
that generates extremely small,
easily metastasized tumors.

00:33:57.233 --> 00:34:00.033
Because it's buried
deeply within the liver,

00:34:00.100 --> 00:34:02.533
it's very hard to detect.

00:34:02.600 --> 00:34:06.467
I went into Cosmic, the compendium
semantic mutations and cancer

00:34:06.533 --> 00:34:10.400
and pulled out the top 20 genes

00:34:10.467 --> 00:34:14.633
were mutations of which were
associated with biliary tract cancer.

00:34:14.700 --> 00:34:18.067
Of those, we were able to
identify the top seven,

00:34:18.133 --> 00:34:20.467
as you can see on the
pie chart on the right,

00:34:20.533 --> 00:34:25.100
including a number of mutations
identified in KMT2C,

00:34:25.167 --> 00:34:30.000
which is a mental transference gene

00:34:30.067 --> 00:34:33.933
that has been associated with this
particular variety of cancer.

00:34:34.000 --> 00:34:36.667
Our TP53 mutation actually generated

00:34:36.733 --> 00:34:40.233
a very short and truncated
version of the P53 protein,

00:34:40.300 --> 00:34:45.400
so it's not surprising that
this tumor grew very rapidly.

00:34:45.533 --> 00:34:47.100
But we were able to identify,

00:34:47.167 --> 00:34:49.567
even from our limited
amount of NextSeq data,

00:34:49.633 --> 00:34:53.567
mutations that were diagnostic
of the type of cancer

00:34:53.633 --> 00:34:56.667
that the pathology
department had assigned

00:34:56.733 --> 00:35:00.967
to that particular variety of cancer.

00:35:01.033 --> 00:35:06.733
So yeah, it was a fun experiment.

00:35:07.667 --> 00:35:09.100
So with that,

00:35:09.167 --> 00:35:11.667
I want to acknowledge my colleagues
here at Beckman Coulter,

00:35:11.733 --> 00:35:13.733
including Shilpa Parakh,

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:17.233
who is also a next generation
applications development scientist

00:35:17.433 --> 00:35:19.400
like myself.

00:35:19.467 --> 00:35:23.100
And Kathy Munkvold, Li Liu, Asmita
Patel, Amy Yoder, and JP Palys,

00:35:23.167 --> 00:35:26.300
who are all members of our
internal development team

00:35:26.367 --> 00:35:28.667
for the FormaPure DNA kit,

00:35:28.733 --> 00:35:33.200
as well as Alisa Jackson, who is
our genomics automation manager.

00:35:33.267 --> 00:35:36.367
With that, I will be happy to
take your questions.

00:35:36.433 --> 00:35:39.467
Thank you very much for attending.

00:35:39.533 --> 00:35:41.500
Thank you, Zach, for
your presentation.

00:35:41.567 --> 00:35:44.300
A quick reminder for our audience
on how to submit questions.

00:35:44.367 --> 00:35:46.367
Simply type them in the Q &amp; A box

00:35:46.433 --> 00:35:48.533
found by clicking on
the green Q &amp; A button

00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:51.100
at the lower left of the
presentation window.

00:35:51.167 --> 00:35:54.633
Zach Smith will answer as many
questions as time permits.

00:35:54.700 --> 00:35:57.033
And our first question is,

00:35:57.100 --> 00:36:02.667
"are all of your demonstrated methods
available with i-Series instruments?"

00:36:03.200 --> 00:36:05.133
So that's a great question.

00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:08.433
We are in the process of moving
our demonstrated methods

00:36:08.500 --> 00:36:11.033
over to the i-Series platforms.

00:36:11.100 --> 00:36:14.967
There's been a number of changes
to the hardware and the software.

00:36:15.033 --> 00:36:18.533
So, for example, the software for
the i-Series runs on Windows 10,

00:36:18.600 --> 00:36:22.067
and the current Biomek
runs on Windows 7.

00:36:22.133 --> 00:36:24.367
So there's a software challenge there.

00:36:24.433 --> 00:36:27.933
And then there's the increased deck
capacity and the new grippers,

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:30.967
for example, of the new platform.

00:36:31.033 --> 00:36:33.200
That is really giving
us an opportunity

00:36:33.267 --> 00:36:35.500
to look at our current
demonstrated methods

00:36:35.567 --> 00:36:39.600
and take advantage of all the new
capabilities we have on the i-Series.

00:36:39.667 --> 00:36:41.600
Can we make the method faster?

00:36:41.667 --> 00:36:43.633
Can we make it process more samples?

00:36:43.700 --> 00:36:45.600
Can we reduce the
number of interactions

00:36:45.667 --> 00:36:47.733
the user needs to do
with the instrument

00:36:47.800 --> 00:36:50.733
in order to get a complete
workflow on the deck?

00:36:50.800 --> 00:36:53.367
We are in the process of building out

00:36:53.433 --> 00:36:57.567
that demonstrated portfolio
for the i- Series right now.

00:36:57.633 --> 00:37:00.200
And we'll be happy to talk

00:37:00.267 --> 00:37:03.667
about any specific methods if
you would want to contact us.

00:37:04.067 --> 00:37:09.333
Next question is, "Can I use
FormaPure DNA for RNA isolation?"

00:37:09.400 --> 00:37:11.300
Yes, you can.

00:37:11.367 --> 00:37:14.367
As we covered in the slide deck,

00:37:14.433 --> 00:37:20.667
we do have an extraction workflow for
extracting RNA from FFPE material

00:37:20.733 --> 00:37:24.100
using our FormaPure DNA kit.

00:37:24.167 --> 00:37:27.333
"Can you provide an example
where an NGS method is shorter

00:37:27.400 --> 00:37:30.167
or has fewer touchpoints
on an i- Series instrument

00:37:30.233 --> 00:37:33.400
compared to current Biomek platforms?"

00:37:34.433 --> 00:37:35.600
Yes, I certainly can.

00:37:35.667 --> 00:37:38.633
So with Illumina stranded
mRNA, for example,

00:37:38.700 --> 00:37:41.667
right now on our FX, there's
a mandatory deck reset

00:37:41.733 --> 00:37:44.267
at the end of CVN synthesis,

00:37:44.333 --> 00:37:48.600
and then you go through and do the
remainder of the library preparation.

00:37:48.667 --> 00:37:54.367
For the Biomek FX, that
whole method runtime

00:37:54.433 --> 00:37:59.067
is approximately ten and
a half hours or so.

00:37:59.133 --> 00:38:02.967
Putting the same method on the
i7, we have enough deck space,

00:38:03.033 --> 00:38:06.200
we can put everything on the deck
that we need for the entirety

00:38:06.267 --> 00:38:08.667
of library construction
from starting with your RNA

00:38:08.733 --> 00:38:12.967
all the way through to the end,
the final enrichment PCR step.

00:38:13.033 --> 00:38:17.167
Because of the extra capacity and
whatnot of the multichannel head,

00:38:17.233 --> 00:38:20.600
we're able to do that
entire 96 sample workflow

00:38:20.667 --> 00:38:22.667
in about eight and a half hours.

00:38:22.733 --> 00:38:25.033
So we've saved one touchpoint

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:28.567
and approximately an hour and
a half of processing time.

00:38:29.267 --> 00:38:32.100
"Has other Beckman software,
SAMI, DART, been updated

00:38:32.167 --> 00:38:35.167
to work with the i-Series instruments?"

00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:36.600
Yes.

00:38:36.667 --> 00:38:38.533
In addition to Biomek,

00:38:38.733 --> 00:38:42.133
we also have DART and
SAMI have been updated

00:38:42.233 --> 00:38:44.133
to work with the new
i-Series platforms.

00:38:44.200 --> 00:38:46.867
For those of you who aren't familiar,

00:38:47.033 --> 00:38:49.433
DART, our data acquisition
and reporting tool,

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:54.100
allows you to take data in and out of
Biomek software from a limb system.

00:38:54.167 --> 00:38:57.633
Best way to think about it is
the front porch of your limbs.

00:38:57.700 --> 00:39:00.200
So if you want to be able to move data

00:39:00.267 --> 00:39:03.767
in and out of the DART
repository between methods

00:39:03.933 --> 00:39:07.533
as the instrument is processing,
that's perfectly fine with DART.

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:09.933
And then SAMI is our larger
scheduling software.

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:13.933
So if you wanted to run multiple runs

00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:16.133
and you wanted to be able to
schedule them efficiently,

00:39:16.200 --> 00:39:20.133
basically runs inside of incubations
while - basically start a method

00:39:20.200 --> 00:39:24.000
and then run another method
inside that incubation period,

00:39:24.067 --> 00:39:27.500
or if you wanted to schedule multiple
runs across multiple instruments,

00:39:27.567 --> 00:39:32.300
SAMI would be your one.

00:39:32.367 --> 00:39:34.700
So yes, it's all been updated

00:39:34.767 --> 00:39:39.633
to run on the new i-Series
software and instruments.

00:39:39.700 --> 00:39:41.567
"Say a variant is not detected,

00:39:41.633 --> 00:39:43.700
do you have to assume the possibility

00:39:43.767 --> 00:39:47.400
that that variant to be less than two
percent and find another method?

00:39:47.467 --> 00:39:49.467
Or just throw away that gene info?

00:39:49.533 --> 00:39:53.267
Or was the example set
up to show this anomaly?"

00:39:53.667 --> 00:39:58.067
So generally, for samples
coming from Horizon,

00:39:58.133 --> 00:40:01.067
I tend to believe that
the variants are there

00:40:01.133 --> 00:40:04.600
when Horizons QC department
says that they're there.

00:40:04.667 --> 00:40:08.600
If the variant falls below two
percent, maybe what you need to do is

00:40:08.667 --> 00:40:12.567
you need to find a different NGS kit
that's sort of amplification based,

00:40:12.633 --> 00:40:16.400
like TruSight Tumor 15
or 26, for example,

00:40:16.467 --> 00:40:20.367
that allows you to do a
higher depth of coverage.

00:40:20.433 --> 00:40:24.733
TruSeq Exome, because you're
sequencing in the entire exome,

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:28.400
is not really the best platform
for putting on the next seq

00:40:28.467 --> 00:40:31.367
just because you're trying to
get a higher depth of coverage

00:40:31.433 --> 00:40:33.733
over a much wider
region of the genome.

00:40:33.800 --> 00:40:39.167
Maybe an amplification based NGS
application would be better

00:40:39.233 --> 00:40:42.333
if you're hunting a small
subsection of genes

00:40:42.400 --> 00:40:45.400
on a smaller sequencing instrument.

00:40:45.733 --> 00:40:51.333
"What sort of equipment integrations
are available for i-Series platform?"

00:40:51.400 --> 00:40:54.433
One of the things thats nice
about the new i-Series platforms

00:40:54.500 --> 00:40:57.467
is the extra space we have
because of the open design

00:40:57.533 --> 00:40:59.633
to allow for more integrations.

00:40:59.700 --> 00:41:06.000
So we have - the plate washers,
we can put plate readers,

00:41:06.200 --> 00:41:08.567
there are a couple of
integrated centrifuge options

00:41:08.633 --> 00:41:11.567
that we can offer as well.

00:41:11.633 --> 00:41:14.067
We can also integrate robotic arms,

00:41:14.133 --> 00:41:16.400
so if you wanted to be
able to pass plates

00:41:16.467 --> 00:41:19.467
in and out of different liquid
handlers, we can certainly do that.

00:41:19.533 --> 00:41:22.333
We actually have an entire
department internally

00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:25.600
whose job it is to integrate
third party devices

00:41:25.667 --> 00:41:27.600
and make them work with our systems.

00:41:27.667 --> 00:41:31.633
So we' d be happy to discuss
your instrument of interest

00:41:31.700 --> 00:41:34.967
in more detail, if you'd like.

00:41:35.033 --> 00:41:37.000
"Could you give some additional detail

00:41:37.067 --> 00:41:39.833
on the depth layout for
library preparation?"

00:41:40.000 --> 00:41:42.133
Certainly.

00:41:42.200 --> 00:41:45.633
I'm just going to slide back
through the presentation real quick

00:41:45.700 --> 00:41:48.933
because we've got a got a great
screen shot of the deck.

00:41:54.267 --> 00:41:57.333
So this is the deck layout that
we're looking at right now

00:41:57.400 --> 00:42:04.433
for sort of our standard NGS
deck for the Biomek i7.

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:07.600
We have the ability to put on
an integrated thermocycler.

00:42:07.667 --> 00:42:09.700
In this case, it's the Thermo ATC,

00:42:09.767 --> 00:42:14.033
which I have on my system in the lab.

00:42:14.100 --> 00:42:16.433
And we have one static Peltier

00:42:16.500 --> 00:42:22.067
in order to keep the enzyme master
mixes cool while they're on the deck.

00:42:22.133 --> 00:42:25.733
We have an orbital shaker
as well for shaking -

00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:29.333
basically, when you do an enzyme
addition, you'll shake the plate,

00:42:29.433 --> 00:42:32.133
or if you're doing
bead cleanups as well,

00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:34.667
we'll use an orbital shaker for that.

00:42:34.733 --> 00:42:37.633
We also have a - not listed
here on the highlight -

00:42:37.700 --> 00:42:39.633
but you also have a
multichannel wash stations.

00:42:39.700 --> 00:42:43.000
So we have the ability to go through
and wash our tips if we want to.

00:42:43.067 --> 00:42:46.667
I also use it for just waste dispense
for non-hazardous chemicals.

00:42:46.733 --> 00:42:49.600
So we'll just dispense waste to there.

00:42:49.667 --> 00:42:52.067
For those of you who are running LDTs

00:42:52.133 --> 00:42:56.033
or sort of near clinical applications,

00:42:56.100 --> 00:42:59.333
obviously, we can incorporate
our automated labware feeders

00:42:59.400 --> 00:43:02.133
that would allow us to bring
more tip boxes onto the deck,

00:43:02.200 --> 00:43:05.667
so we're not washing tips.

00:43:05.733 --> 00:43:08.967
And then we have our seven
tip loader positions.

00:43:09.033 --> 00:43:12.967
As I mentioned earlier, the i-Series
does not have a pneumatic tip loader

00:43:13.033 --> 00:43:15.233
like the Biomex FX does.

00:43:15.300 --> 00:43:17.200
We're basically just
using press loading

00:43:17.267 --> 00:43:21.300
to load the tips for our multichannel
for these defined tip loader positions.

00:43:21.367 --> 00:43:24.033
So you can have as many
of those as you want.

00:43:24.100 --> 00:43:26.567
Its really convenient
because you don't have to -

00:43:26.633 --> 00:43:28.633
if you have enough tip
loader positions,

00:43:28.700 --> 00:43:31.267
you don't have to move the tip
boxes around to a location

00:43:31.333 --> 00:43:34.600
and then wait for them to load.
You just load and go.

00:43:34.667 --> 00:43:38.267
The other thing you'll see here is I have,
in addition to all that other stuff,

00:43:38.333 --> 00:43:42.433
27 static positions for holding
tips for the Span-8 pod

00:43:42.500 --> 00:43:44.533
and labware plates and reservoirs.

00:43:44.600 --> 00:43:47.467
It really is a very nice system.

00:43:47.533 --> 00:43:51.367
It's making me very lazy as
an applications developer.

00:43:53.367 --> 00:43:55.000
Well, thank you.

